<Layout
  title="Do You Actually Need Paid Antivirus, or Is Windows Defender Enough?"
  description="What independent lab tests actually show about Windows Defender's protection scores, the real gaps that remain, and who genuinely still needs a paid product."
>
  <ArticleHeader
    category="Software"
    title="Do You Actually Need Paid Antivirus, or Is Windows Defender Enough?"
      updated="September 2026"
  />

  <p>
    For years, "just use a real antivirus, not the built-in one" was safe
    default advice. That's no longer quite true, and pretending otherwise
    is exactly the kind of outdated advice that gets recycled without
    anyone checking the current lab results.
  </p>

  <p class="callout">
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> Windows Defender's actual malware
    protection is now genuinely competitive with paid antivirus, according
    to independent testing labs. What paid products still offer isn't
    better core protection so much as extra features Defender doesn't
    have at all.
  </p>

  <h2>What the independent labs actually show</h2>

  <p>
    AV-TEST, one of the most established independent testing labs, has
    given Windows Defender its maximum score — six out of six — in recent
    testing rounds. AV-Comparatives, another major independent lab, places
    it at or near the top of the field in real-world protection testing.
    This isn't a marginal "it's finally acceptable" result; on core
    malware detection, Defender is now performing at the same level as
    the paid products it used to trail badly.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the real gaps still are</h2>

  <p>
    The remaining differences aren't really about catching more malware —
    they're specific and worth knowing:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Browser-specific phishing protection.</strong> Defender's
      SmartScreen protection works well in Microsoft Edge, but is
      meaningfully weaker in Chrome or Firefox. If you don't use Edge,
      this is a real gap, not a theoretical one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Zero-day detection lags slightly.</strong> Against
      brand-new, previously unseen threats, some paid competitors still
      edge Defender out — the gap has narrowed a lot, but it isn't zero.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No bundled extras.</strong> Defender doesn't include a
      password manager, VPN, identity monitoring, parental controls, or a
      secure banking browser the way many paid suites do. If you want
      those bundled together, that's a real reason to pay — just make
      sure you'd actually use them, rather than paying for a bundle to get
      the antivirus part alone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It's a bigger target precisely because it's popular.</strong>
      Attackers spend real effort building workarounds specifically for
      the antivirus running on the most PCs.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who should still consider paying</h2>

  <p>
    Paid antivirus still makes sense if you primarily browse in Chrome or
    Firefox rather than Edge, handle sensitive professional data, want
    the bundled extras rolled into one subscription, share a computer with
    someone less careful about what they click, or regularly download from
    unofficial sources. If none of that describes you, Defender's current
    lab scores don't really support paying just for "better protection" —
    that specific gap has largely closed.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest verdict</h2>

  <p>
    Don't pay for antivirus out of habit or outdated advice. Check what
    you're actually browsing in, whether you'd use the bundled extras, and
    whether your own habits (unofficial downloads, shared devices) put you
    in the group that still benefits. For a lot of people, that's a short
    list — and Defender, already running for free on every Windows PC,
    covers the rest.
  </p>
</Layout>
